From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:59:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: hand Blink's downloads to Chrome's download UI

ContentBrowserContext had no DownloadManagerDelegate, so content gave
every download an empty target path, which cancels it: a link to a file,
or to a PDF (which Blink on iOS cannot display), did nothing.

Add a delegate that wraps each download in a ContentDownloadTask, a
web::DownloadTask that Chrome's download bar shows and runs like a
WKWebView download. When the user taps Download, the task hands the
path Chrome picked to content as the download target, and it reports
content's progress back. Dismissing the bar, stopping the download or
closing the tab cancels it in content. DownloadControllerImpl gets a
public entry point for such tasks. The delegate detaches itself from
content's download manager when destroyed, since content::BrowserContext
shuts the manager down after ContentBrowserContext's members are gone.

For a finished PDF, Chrome offers "Open", which loads the file in a new
tab. Blink has no PDF viewer, so that tab only downloaded the file
again; with Blink, offer "Open in..." for PDFs as for other files.

Status: verified at Chromium 4558926c in the iOS 27 simulator. A link
to a ZIP file, a link with a download attribute, a link to a PDF and a
download of a page-generated blob each show Chrome's download bar, and
after Download the file in Documents matches the original byte for
byte. Dismissing the bar before Download, stopping a download midway
and closing its tab each end the transfer (the test server sees the
connection close) and leave no partial file. "Open in..." shows the
share sheet, also for a PDF. Not tested on a device yet.

